WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.440
He was written into "The Big Lebowski"&nbsp;
without his knowledge, and he's officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.240
in the Mustache Hall of Fame. Keep watching for so&nbsp;
much more about the untold truth of Sam Elliott.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.240
Born in 1944 in Sacramento, California,&nbsp;
Sam Elliott began acting in the late '60s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.480
He did what a lot of actors before him had done:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.400
He started out in small roles in television&nbsp;
Westerns, eventually earning slightly more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.520
substantial parts in the hit shows&nbsp;
"Lancer" and "Mission: Impossible."

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.680
His first role in a major film came in&nbsp;
1969's "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.320
Kid," which featured the superstar leads Paul&nbsp;
Newman and Robert Redford in the title roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.880
A comedic Western based on the lives of two&nbsp;
notorious bank-robbing outlaws, the film seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.200
like a perfect fit for Elliott, who spent much of&nbsp;
his career working within the genre. Unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.920
for him, he had a blink-and-you'll-miss-it part&nbsp;
as a card player. He didn't even have any lines.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.440
While the role didn't have much of an impact&nbsp;
on his career, the movie did have a rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.760
coincidental place in his life. Starring&nbsp;
beside Newman and Redford was Katharine Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.360
and though she and Elliott shared no scenes&nbsp;
and didn't cross paths during filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.000
nearly ten years later, they did meet again and&nbsp;
start dating as co-leads in "The Legacy." It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.160
wasn't long before the couple were married. Today,&nbsp;
they're still together, and they have a daughter.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.200
Over the course of the 1970s and 1980s,&nbsp;
Sam Elliott never quite hit it big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.280
but he did enjoy increasing recognition as a&nbsp;
character actor appearing in television shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.440
TV movies, and big screen productions.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.560
His first major starring role in a feature film&nbsp;
came in 1976's "Lifeguard," which tells the tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.800
of a lifeguard working in Southern California&nbsp;
who's forced into self-reflection after attending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.560
a high school reunion and realizing that his&nbsp;
classmates have accomplished greater things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.560
with their lives. While the movie performed&nbsp;
decently in the box office, Elliott publicly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.560
complained about the film's marketing during&nbsp;
the promotional tour, which caused the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.280
heads at Paramount to become displeased. As a&nbsp;
result, he never worked with the company again.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.760
That wasn't the only trouble he experienced&nbsp;
during this period. During an interview in 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.840
Elliott revealed that he'd been propositioned&nbsp;
repeatedly during auditions, saying,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.240
"You cannot believe the casting&nbsp;
couch stories I could tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.480
man. The clichés are all true. I've had&nbsp;
propositions from men and women, and I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.560
turned them all down. It's probably hurt me,&nbsp;
but I'm the one who has to live with that guilt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.480
My conscience is clear, even though my career&nbsp;
is still not setting the world on fire."

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.640
Perhaps unsurprisingly, he later became&nbsp;
a vocal supporter of the Me Too movement.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.440
However, these decades weren't all hard times,&nbsp;
as he scored a number of high-profile roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.560
In 1985, he starred in a supporting role&nbsp;
alongside Cher in "Mask," then in 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.600
he starred next to Patrick Swayze&nbsp;
in the cult classic "Road House."

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.360
By the time the 1990s rolled around, Sam Elliott&nbsp;
had spent more than 20 years in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.160
but he still didn't have much&nbsp;
in the way of name recognition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.480
For the most part, he seemed destined to forever&nbsp;
be a somewhat acknowledged face without a name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.640
With the arrival of his third decade in the&nbsp;
business, however, that was about to change.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.640
After playing a substantial part in the&nbsp;
critically acclaimed civil war epic "Gettysburg,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.400
Elliott starred alongside Kurt Russell&nbsp;
in the now-classic Western "Tombstone,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.480
which was hailed by audiences and critics&nbsp;
alike. In the role of Virgil Earp — from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.480
famous real-life Old West family of lawmen&nbsp;
headed by the renowned Wyatt Earp — Elliott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.440
was particularly captivating. This helped to&nbsp;
propel him into prominent parts in future films.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.800
Sadly enough, Elliott's father, who&nbsp;
never wanted him to become an actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.520
didn't get a chance to see his success begin. The&nbsp;
two sparred over the career choice and were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.160
able to settle their differences, as his father&nbsp;
died of a heart attack when Elliott was just 18.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.920
Many years later while speaking in an&nbsp;
interview with The Star, Elliott explained,

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.040
"He died thinking, 'Man, this kid is&nbsp;
going to go down the wrong path.' And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.160
I think on some levels that was&nbsp;
either hard on me or made me more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.480
focused in my resolve to have a&nbsp;
career, you know what I mean?"

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.560
These days, "The Big Lebowski" is almost&nbsp;
universally considered one of the Coen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.280
brothers' greatest films, and one of its many&nbsp;
iconic characters is Elliott's "The Stranger,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.560
a surreal narrator who delivers some&nbsp;
of the movie's most quotable lines.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.760
"Darkness washed over the Dude. Darker than a&nbsp;
back steer's tookus on a moonless prairie night."

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.440
According to an interview in The New York&nbsp;
Times, Elliott was initially disappointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.600
at being cast as yet another cowboy.&nbsp;
That was until he read the script.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.880
Speaking to NPR, Elliott explained that&nbsp;
as he looked through the screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.440
he spotted a surprise. As he explained it:

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.880
"Over the dialogue, there's this tumbling&nbsp;
tumbleweed blowing and talk about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.000
Southwest accent and, 'Sounding not unlike&nbsp;
Sam Elliott.' They had my name in the script."

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.160
Later as they were filming his final scene,&nbsp;
Elliott became exasperated by going through take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.160
after take. The Coen brothers finally admitted&nbsp;
that they had already gotten what they wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.400
but they enjoyed watching and listening to him&nbsp;
so much that they had him do it again and again.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.760
"Well, that about does her. Wraps her all up."

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.440
Part of what helped propel Sam Elliott to success&nbsp;
was his distinctive voice, and over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.920
he's lent it to a wide variety of high-profile&nbsp;
commercials. Perhaps the most notable products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.320
you might've heard his familiar voice hawking&nbsp;
are Coors beer and Dodge Ram Heavy Duty trucks.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.760
Since 2008, Elliott has also voiced Smokey&nbsp;
Bear, the U.S. Forest Service mascot created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.760
to help with wildfire prevention efforts.&nbsp;
You probably have heard his catch phrase:

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.760
"Only you can prevent wildfires."

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.360
Another fun fact: Elliott not only&nbsp;
delivers Smokey's famous catchphrase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.080
but he actually shares his birthdate with&nbsp;
the fictional spokesbear: August 9, 1944.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.560
While Sam Elliott's star had been rising steadily&nbsp;
for some 20 years or so, in the 2010s, he really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.800
took off. By the middle of the decade, he had&nbsp;
recurring roles in the Netflix series "The Ranch"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.600
and "Grace and Frankie". He also lent his voice&nbsp;
to the 2015 animated film "The Good Dinosaur."

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.880
Then in 2017, Elliott starred in "The Hero," a&nbsp;
film about an aging Western star whose best days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.960
are behind him. Not only was it the perfect role&nbsp;
for him, but according to the film's writer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.240
director, Brett Haley, it was literally written&nbsp;
for Elliott. As Haley explained to the L.A. Times,

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.120
"To me, it was a love letter not only to Sam, but&nbsp;
to a dying breed of actor and film in Hollywood."

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.680
Shortly after that Elliott starred in the flat-out&nbsp;
bizarre B-film "The Man Who Killed Hitler and Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.600
the Bigfoot," which fared well with critics&nbsp;
thanks largely to Elliott's performance.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.680
In 2018, Sam Elliott hit another major high point&nbsp;
in his career so far with his performance in "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.560
Star Is Born." Directed by Bradley Cooper&nbsp;
and starring Cooper, Elliot, and Lady Gaga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.800
the movie is about the relationship between an&nbsp;
up-and-coming singer and her alcoholic mentor.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.000
"A Star Is Born" was a massive success by&nbsp;
every measure, earning $436 million on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.360
$36 million budget. It garnered almost universal&nbsp;
praise from the critics, and scored eight Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.240
nominations including Best Picture, one win for&nbsp;
Best Original Song, and one nomination for Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.120
Supporting Actor for Sam Elliott. Shockingly,&nbsp;
this was his first Academy Award nomination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.200
indicating that even at 75 years old, his&nbsp;
career still had exciting places to go.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.680
Elliott's performance in the film also earned&nbsp;
him a nomination for Outstanding Performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.160
by a Male Actor in a Supporting Role at the&nbsp;
Screen Actors Guild Award and a win for Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.560
Supporting Actor at the National Board of&nbsp;
Review Awards. Not bad for an old cowboy.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.840
Along with his smooth, deep voice and tough&nbsp;
demeanor, Sam Elliott is best recognized by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.480
his iconic mustache, and we're not&nbsp;
the only ones who think so. In 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.960
Elliott was inducted into the Mustache Hall of&nbsp;
Fame, alongside the likes of real-life legends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.680
such as Teddy Roosevelt, Hulk Hogan, and Billy&nbsp;
Dee Williams, as well as fictional characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.560
such as Ned Flanders and Yosemite Sam. Talk&nbsp;
about a distinguished class of facial hair.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.640
While we all know and love his superior&nbsp;
upper-lip sweater, Elliott himself doesn't

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.445
quite understand all the fuss.

00:07:08.445 --> 00:07:13.165
Sam Elliott might not get all the attention,&nbsp;
but we totally do. That's some ferocious facial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:17.245
fuzz he's got there. Of course, these days&nbsp;
Sam Elliott is among the most recognizable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.245 --> 00:07:21.885
faces in the industry, as he continues to&nbsp;
shine on screens big and small. His latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.885 --> 00:07:26.765
breakout hit is "1883" on Paramount+,&nbsp;
a prequel to the series "Yellowstone."

